{"id":"qzpyq-chemistry-chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure-chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure-test-7","_quizrrTestId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","title":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure - Test 7","questions":[{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f00e","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f00e","q":"The total number of lone pairs of electrons on oxygen atoms of ozone is","options":["","","",""],"answer":"6","answers":["6"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-310670480d27e5d8.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 171px; height: 80px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-310670480d27e5d8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-310670480d27e5d8.png\"><br  />From the above Lewis structure of ozone we can see,</p><p>There are three oxygen atoms having two and three lone pairs on terminal oxygen atoms and one lone pair central oxygen atom respectively.</p><p>Total number of lone pairs on oxygen atoms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p>&nbsp;</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"6","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f013","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f013","q":"Number of molecules/species from the following having one unpaired electron is \\(\\qquad\\) \\(\\mathrm{O}_2, \\mathrm{O}_2^{-1}, \\mathrm{NO}, \\mathrm{CN}^{-1}, \\mathrm{O}_2^{2-}\\)","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"According to M.O.T.\n$\\mathrm{O}_2 \\rightarrow$ no. of unpaired electrons $=2\\mathrm{O}_2^{-} \\rightarrow$ no. of unpaired electron $=1\\mathrm{NO} \\rightarrow$ no. of unpaired electron $=1\\mathrm{CN}^{-} \\rightarrow$ no. of unpaired electron $=0\\mathrm{O}_2^{2-} \\rightarrow$ no. of unpaired electron $=0$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"2.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f018","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f018","q":"The total number of 'Sigma' and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Pi</mi></math> bonds in 2-formylhex-4-enoic acid is","options":["","","",""],"answer":"22","answers":["22"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>A single bond is a result of the axial overlap of bonding orbitals. Hence, it contributes a sigma bond. A multiple bond (double or triple bond) contain one sigma bond and the remaining are pi bonds. </p><p>The total number of sigma and pi bond in </p><p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>-Formyl hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>-enoic acid is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>22</mn></math>.</p><p>The compound can be represented as,</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-2279476860db6ca1.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 482px; height: 206px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2279476860db6ca1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2279476860db6ca1.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"22","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f01d","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f01d","q":"The number of species below that have two lone pairs of electrons in their central atom is ___ (Round off to the Nearest integer)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>BF</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ClF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>AsF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>PCl</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/c613a479-e58c-4341-9f97-b6f4912902f6-image.png\" style=\"width: 389px; height: 467px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-eb5a79df08573ec8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-eb5a79df08573ec8.png\"></p><p>Two l.p. on central atom is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ClF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p><p>The valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR) theory is a model used to predict 3-D molecular geometry based on the number of valence shell electron bond pairs among the atoms in a molecule or ion. This model assumes that electron pairs will arrange themselves to minimize repulsion effects from one another.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"2.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f022","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f022","q":"<p>Amongst the following the number of oxide(s) which are paramagnetic in nature is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ClO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>NO</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":"4","answers":["4"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The magnetic properties of a substance can be determined by examining its electron configuration: If it has unpaired electrons, then the substance is paramagnetic and if all electrons are paired, the substance is then diamagnetic.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is potassium superoxide. There is one unpaired electron in anti-bonding molecular orbital of superoxide. Potassium superoxide is paramagnetic due to the presence of unpaired electron.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ClO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>NO</mi></math>&nbsp;are odd electron species. Hence, they are paramagnetic.</p><p>The other are monoxide and dioxide contain paired electrons, so&nbsp;they&nbsp;will show diamagnetic behaviour.</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"4","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f027","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f027","q":"The number of species trom the following carrying a single lone pair on central atom Xenon is<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>XeF</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>XeF</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-509ebf38eba0a94c.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 280px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-509ebf38eba0a94c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-509ebf38eba0a94c.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-340c475eeb812069.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 303px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-340c475eeb812069.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-340c475eeb812069.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-e2c7000f3db05a49.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 348px; height: 357px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e2c7000f3db05a49.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e2c7000f3db05a49.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-58a28f1e52200217.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 211px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-58a28f1e52200217.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-58a28f1e52200217.png\"></p><p>The central <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Xe</mi></math> atom in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> has three bonding domains and one&nbsp;lone pair&nbsp;of electrons. Hence, the&nbsp;electron geometry is tetrahedral and molecular geometry is pyramidal.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is&nbsp;trigonal bipyramidal&nbsp;and the shape is a see-saw.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is in&nbsp;square pyramidal shape.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>XeF</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;have single lone pair on central atom.</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"4.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f02c","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f02c","q":"According to molecular orbital theory, the number of unpaired electron(s) in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> is _________ .","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;there is no unpaired electron<br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"0.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f031","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f031","q":"<p>The number of molecule(s) or ion(s) from the following having non-planar structure is______.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>PCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>PH</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Al</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo></msup></math></p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The species which have&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;hybridisation with no lone pairs,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;hybridisation with two lone pairs are planar molecules.</p><p>The number of hybrid orbitals can be calculated as follows,</p><p>No.of hybrid orbitals = 1/2(V+M-C+A)</p><p>V = number of valence electrons</p><p>M = number of monovalent atoms attached to central atom.</p><p>C = Number of positive charges on the molecule</p><p>A =&nbsp;Number of negative charges on the molecule</p><p>&nbsp;</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 100%;\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Al</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo></msup></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>PH</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></math></td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></td>  <td>Non-Planar</td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"6.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f036","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f036","q":"Total number of electrons present in \\(\\left(\\pi^*\\right)\\) molecular orbitals of \\(\\mathrm{O}_2, \\mathrm{O}_2{ }^{+}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{O}_2^{-}\\) is _______.","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{O}_2(16 \\mathrm{e}):\\left(\\sigma_{1 \\mathrm{~s}}\\right)^2\\left(\\sigma_{1 \\mathrm{~s}}^*\\right)^2\\left(\\sigma_{2 \\mathrm{~s}}\\right)^2\\left(\\sigma_{2 \\mathrm{~s}}^*\\right)^2 \\\\\n& \\left(\\sigma_{2 \\mathrm{p}}\\right)^2\\left[\\left(\\pi_{2 \\mathrm{p}}\\right)^2=\\left(\\pi_{2 \\mathrm{p}}\\right)^2\\right],\\left[\\left(\\pi_{2 \\mathrm{p}}^*\\right)^1=\\left(\\mathrm{m}_{2 \\mathrm{p}}^*\\right)^1\\right]\n\\end{aligned}$<br  />Number of $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$present in $\\left(\\pi^*\\right)$ of $\\mathrm{O}_2=2$\nNumber of $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$present in $\\left(\\pi^*\\right)$ of $\\mathrm{O}_2^{+}=1$\nNumber of $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$present in $\\left(\\pi^*\\right)$ of $\\mathrm{O}_2^{-}=3$\nSo total $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$in $\\left(\\pi^*\\right)=2+1+3=6$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"6.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f03b","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f03b","q":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AB</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is an interhalogen T-shaped molecule. The number of lone pairs of electrons on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is (Integer answer)","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>In interhalogen compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AB</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;Central atom A is halogen so it contain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn></math> electrons in its outer most shell. so its structure is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0c28e8982d984eb8.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 199px; height: 156px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0c28e8982d984eb8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0c28e8982d984eb8.png\"></p><p>So central atom A contain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pairs of electrons.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"2.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f040","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f040","q":"The total number of molecular orbitals formed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> atomic orbitals of a diatomic molecule","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>2s and 2p atomic orbitals combine, forming σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals. σ orbitals result from head-on overlap, while π orbitals arise from sideways overlap. This molecular orbital theory explains the bonding in molecules</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn></mn></math><mo> </mo><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital will form, two molecular orbitals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>.<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> orbital will form six molecular orbitals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math></p><p>Hence the total number of molecular orbitals formed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> atomic orbitals of a diatomic molecule formed is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"8.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f045","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f045","q":"The total number of electrons in all bonding molecular orbitals of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> is ......... (Round off to the nearest integer)","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>M. O. Configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> ( <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>18</mn><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>&#175;</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#963;</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#963;</mi><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>Total B.M.O electrons <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"10.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f04a","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f04a","q":"<p>The number of species from the following which have square pyramidal structure is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>BrF</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>IF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ICl</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math></p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>Among the given,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>IF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;have squre pyramidal structure.&nbsp; VSEPR&nbsp;theory helps us to predict the shape of molecules from the number of electron pairs and lone pairs of electrons that surrounds the central atoms.&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-11448cb43ae3f9f5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 188px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-11448cb43ae3f9f5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-11448cb43ae3f9f5.png\"> <img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-8a722efb578d327a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 207px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8a722efb578d327a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8a722efb578d327a.png\">&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-bda4261f9c6b34b5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 183px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bda4261f9c6b34b5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bda4261f9c6b34b5.png\"></p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"3.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f04f","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f04f","q":"Total number of molecules/species from following which will be paramagnetic is ______ \n$\\mathrm{O}_2, \\mathrm{O}_2^{+}, \\mathrm{O}_2^{-}, \\mathrm{NO}, \\mathrm{NO}_2, \\mathrm{CO}, \\mathrm{K}_2\\left[\\mathrm{NiCl}_4\\right],\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_3\\right)_6\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_3, \\mathrm{~K}_2\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CN})_4\\right]$","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\mathrm{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2$ unpaired electrons according to MOT\n$\\mathrm{O}_2^{+} \\rightarrow 1$ unpaired electrons according to MOT\n$\\mathrm{O}_2^{-} \\rightarrow 1$ unpaired electrons according to MOT\n$\\mathrm{NO} \\rightarrow$ odd electron species\n$\\mathrm{NO}_2 \\rightarrow$ odd electron species\n$\\mathrm{K}_2\\left[\\mathrm{NiCl}_4\\right] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \\Rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~d}^8$ weak Ligand, C.N. $=4\\Rightarrow$ Tetrahedral, Paramagnetic with 2 unpaired electrons","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"6.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f054","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f054","q":"The hybridization of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> exhibited in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math>&nbsp;is","options":["","","",""],"answer":"1","answers":["1"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"The hybridization is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> hybridization and phosphorous atom forms five <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> hybrid orbitals. Five hybrid orbitals will be used to form bonds with five fluorine atoms. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math> sigma bonds in this compound.<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> hybridisation</p><p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;sigma bonds, zero lone pair on central atom) Value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":1,"_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_via_unique_stem","_marksId":"648b5a6e417cc3fb48d67d07"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f059","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f059","q":"<p>The number of molecules from the following which contain only two lone pair of electrons is _____________</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>CO</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>NO</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":"3.0","answers":["3.0"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The lone pair of electrons can be identified by drawing structure of the given moleules as follows,</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-08f114de941b3e5a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 526px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-08f114de941b3e5a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-08f114de941b3e5a.png\"></p><p>The number of molecules having only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pair of electrons&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>Which are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math>.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;lone pairs&nbsp;on central atom, but we are asked about lone pair in molecule</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"3.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f05e","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f05e","q":"The total number of species from the following in which one unpaired electron is present, is _______\n\\(\\mathrm{N}_2, \\mathrm{O}_2, \\mathrm{C}_2^{-}, \\mathrm{O}_2^{-}, \\mathrm{O}_2^{2-}, \\mathrm{H}_2^{+}, \\mathrm{CN}^{-}, \\mathrm{He}_2^{+}\\)","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"One unpaired $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$is present in : $\\mathrm{C}_2^{-} ; \\mathrm{O}_2^{-} ; \\mathrm{H}_2^{+} ; \\mathrm{He}_2^{+}$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"4.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f063","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f063","q":"<p>Consider, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>PCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msub><mi>ICl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ClF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>IF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></math></p><p>Amongst the above molecule(s) ion(s), the number of molecule(s)/ion(s) having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> hybridisation is</p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>Number of hybrid orbitals = Number of bond pair + number of lone pair on central atom.</p><p>To have&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;hybridisation there should be total of 6 (bond pairs + lone pairs). The structure in such case will be octahedral or its derivative structures.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/fdb78405-51a3-4feb-9954-0f62ed23d690-993f7d80-abbd-4917-8eae-e4bac022163f-image.png\" style=\"width: 324px; height: 458px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9b87f88d9c57cc2b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9b87f88d9c57cc2b.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/7b2bff78-da63-4143-99b3-e0bcbb66ef66-9e3bfbe6-4f29-4113-adf7-b70e242753b6-image.png\" style=\"width: 296px; height: 394px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3e63ae7b91683523.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3e63ae7b91683523.png\"></p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"4.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f068","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f068","q":"The total number of anti bonding molecular orbitals, formed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></math> atomic orbitals in a diatomic molecule is _____________.","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The atomic orbitals combine to form a new set of equivalent orbitals known as hybrid orbitals. Unlike pure orbitals, hybrid orbitals are used in bond formation. The phenomenon is known as hybridisation, which can be defined as the process of intermixing of the orbitals of slightly different energies to redistribute their energies, resulting in the formation of a new set of orbitals of equivalent energies and shape.</p><p>The number of hybrid orbitals is equal to the number of atomic orbitals that get hybridised.</p><p>Antibonding molecular orbital from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math></p><p>Antibonding molecular orbital from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math> that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></math>.</p><p>Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"4.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f202ee0e063d923f06d","_quizrrId":"69de4f202ee0e063d923f06d","q":"<p>Amongst the following, the number of species having the linear shape is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>BCl</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8854;</mo></msubsup></math></p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><br  />Using VSEPR theory, following are the shapes: <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1c85bb32be8ebfc2.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 600px; height: 317px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1c85bb32be8ebfc2.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1c85bb32be8ebfc2.png\"></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;have linear structure.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f37e39d99b57bc5b39","correctValue":"5.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"}]}